course: dictionary
lesson: 1484
title: Cauldron: a pot, a place, a stadium
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cauldron is a very large, deep metal pot. Words around a new word are its context, and they leave clues. «Pour vinegar onto a heap of baking soda and it froths up like a witch's cauldron.» Froths means bubbles up, so the cauldron must be a pot that bubbles.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «The crowd made the stadium a cauldron of noise.» A stadium cannot be a metal pot. Here cauldron means a place full of wild noise and excitement. Ask: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Sometimes the sentence gives you the meaning straight away. «The cook stirs a large cauldron (a big cooking pot) of soup.» The words in brackets explain the hard word for you. Authors often do this to help readers.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Cauldron» means a very large, deep metal pot for cooking over a fire.
fact: «Cauldron» can also mean a place or situation full of wild feelings or activity, as in «a cauldron of noise».
fact: In sport, the Olympic cauldron is the large bowl in which the Olympic flame burns.
fact: In US English this word is spelled «caldron», without the u.
exercise: «The cook stirs a large cauldron of soup.» What is the cauldron here? | A place full of noise | *A big cooking pot | A type of baby | A kind of stool
exercise: «The crowd made the stadium a cauldron of noise.» What does cauldron mean here? | *A place full of wild noise and excitement | A big metal pot for soup | A quiet library | A kind of stool
exercise: In the Olympics, what is the cauldron? | *The large bowl in which the Olympic flame burns | A torch carried by runners | A cooking pot for soup | A kind of stadium seat
test: Witches in stories often stir a bubbling cauldron of potion. What does bubbling tell you about the cauldron? | *Hot and moving, with bubbles rising | Cold and empty | Made of wood | Very small
test: Ella says cauldron means a cold, quiet room. Why is she wrong? | Cauldron means the sea | *The word means a big deep pot, or a place full of wild activity, not a quiet room | Cauldron means a quiet room, so she is right | Cauldron is another word for a stool
exam: Maya reads «the classroom was a cauldron of excitement» and says cauldron must mean a pot of soup. What is the best answer? | *No, here it means a place full of wild excitement, and a pot would not fit the sentence | Yes, because classrooms are full of soup | No, it means the classroom is cold | Yes, because cauldron is always a pot
